I was triaging an issue caused by this when working on a bug report.

If you load a TypeScript file through `import` or `await import("…")` then any loaded modules have an undefined `require.cache` even if they're CJS. If you load a module through `createRequire(…)` then it and any of its transitive modules will have a `require.cache`.

```js
// test.js
import "./file-1.ts";

// or this in either esm or cjs files:
// import("./file-1.ts");
```

```ts
// file-1.ts
require("./file-2.cjs");

// This also errors in this file:
// delete require.cache["foo"];

// this works:
// require("node:module").createRequire(__filename)("./file-2.cjs");
```

```ts
// file-2.cjs
// error happens here because of the import in `test.js`
delete require.cache["foo"];
```

Loading a typescript file through `require(…)` works as expected.
